ed method is checked against what the extension was granted at install; a denial answers `PERMISSION_DENIED`. These methods are their own set, never a way into the application's commands. The one way into the application's own functions is the management catalog (`tools.list`, `tools.call`): the same tools the built-in Agent and MCP clients use, behind the `management` permission, each change waiting for the user.","locale":"en"},"4896":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-105","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"Method","locale":"en"},"4897":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-106","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"Needs","locale":"en"},"4898":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-107","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`host.info`, `host.preferences`, `runtime.status`, `log.write`, `settings.get`, `settings.patch`, `storage.path`, `ui.notify`, `ui.progress`, `ui.prompt`, the notifications `ui.frame.emit` and `ui.emit`","locale":"en"},"4899":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-108","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"Nothing","locale":"en"},"4900":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-109","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`instances.list`, `instances.get`","locale":"en"},"4901":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-110","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`instances = \"read\"` or `\"all\"`","locale":"en"},"4902":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-111","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`instances.ofType`, `instances.configuration`, `servers.status`","locale":"en"},"4903":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-112","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`instances = \"own-types\"` or `\"all\"`; its own types' instances only","locale":"en"},"4904":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-113","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`runtime.resolve`","locale":"en"},"4905":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-114","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`processes = true`","locale":"en"},"4906":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-115","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`process.run`, `process.cancel`","locale":"en"},"4907":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-116","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`processes = true`; the working directory inside its data directory, or with `filesystem = \"own-instances\"` one of its own instances' directories","locale":"en"},"4908":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-117","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`http.request`, `download.fetch`","locale":"en"},"4909":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-118","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"Both require a URL host allowed by `network` and use the application’s proxy. A download’s destination must be in the extension’s data directory.","locale":"en"},"4910":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-119","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`events.subscribe`","locale":"en"},"4911":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-120","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`settings` and `preferences` require no additional permission. `instance` needs any `instances` level except `none` (`own-types` sees only its own types). `server` needs `own-types` or `all`. `task` needs `management = \"confirm\"`.","locale":"en"},"4912":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-121","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`python.ensure`","locale":"en"},"4913":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-122","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`processes = true`; the destination strictly below its data directory, or with `own-instances` in one of its own instances' directories","locale":"en"},"4914":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-123","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`git.clone`, `git.pull`","locale":"en"},"4915":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-124","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`processes = true`; a remote host matching `network`, or a `file` URL of a repository inside what it may use; destinations and directories as above","locale":"en"},"4916":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-125","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`services.call`","locale":"en"},"4917":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-126","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"A dependency on the target in its manifest, and the method in the target's `exports`","locale":"en"},"4918":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-127","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`instances.scan`","locale":"en"},"4919":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-128","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`registerInstances = true`","locale":"en"},"4920":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-129","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`instances.import`","locale":"en"},"4921":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-130","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`registerInstances = true`, and `instances = \"own-types\"` (its own types only) or `\"all\"` (any type); with `none` or `read` it is denied","locale":"en"},"4922":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-131","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`tools.list`, `tools.call`","locale":"en"},"4923":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-132","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`management = \"read\"` (the read tools) or `\"confirm\"` (every tool, each change once the user allows it)","locale":"en"},"4924":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-133","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"In practice:","locale":"en"},"4925":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-134","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`host.info` answers the application's version, the protocol version, the locale, the platform (`linux`, `macos`, `windows`), `instancesDir`, the folder new instances go in by default, and `mirrors`, the user's download mirrors as Settings has them now: `automatic` (the user leaves the mirrors to `sd_webui_all_in_one`'s detection, its `auto_mirror`, with the switches below as its input) and `pypi`, `github`, `huggingface`, each `{ enabled, custom }` (`custom` the user's own mirror URL or `null`; PyPI has none). Use `download.fetch` with an explicit `source` to apply the host mirror policy; `direct` keeps the supplied URL. A script doing its own networking must apply its own policy. `settings.get` answers the extension's settings values; `settings.patch {patch}` updates only its declared fields and returns canonical `{values}`; `storage.path` its data directory.","locale":"en"},"4926":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-135","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`log.write` may be sent as a call or a notification, at `error`, `warn`, `info` (the default), or `debug`.","locale":"en"},"4927":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-136","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`instances.list` and `instances.get` answer read-only instances: `id`, `name`, `type`, `path`, `running`, and `components` (by name, each `{ path, external }`: the folder as the platform writes it, and whether it is a folder of the user's; empty when the instance's components cannot be resolved). Read them from `instances.get` in a frame's or panel's call, which names only the instance: they follow the user's Paths changes. `servers.status` answers each server role's `instanceId`, `role`, `status` (`stopped`, `starting`, `running`, `restarting`, `stopping`, `failed`), and `endpoint`.","locale":"en"},"4928":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-137","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`process.run` takes `argv` (no shell), a `cwd`, and `env` added to the cleaned environment, and answers a `taskId` at once; its output arrives as `process.output` notifications and its end as `process.exit`. `process.cancel` stops it. These processes stop with the backend. `useHostProxy: true` explicitly gives this child the current host proxy environment, overriding matching `env` keys; the default is `false`. This opt-in lets the child read those proxy values, including any embedded credentials. The task detail retains stdout and stderr for viewing, copying and export. Set `instanceId` to an instance of your type to show this process in its management window.","locale":"en"},"4929":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-138","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`download.fetch` takes `url`, optional `sha256`, and `destination`, and returns `{path, sha256}`. Optional `source` is `direct` (default), `github`, or `huggingface`; explicit sources must match the URL and use the current host mirror policy. `cache: true` reuses digest-verified content only when `sha256` is supplied; without a digest it downloads normally. A cache hit is copied into the extension destination; no cache path or write access is exposed. A failed download does not replace an existing destination. Task Center records the transferred bytes, using an indeterminate bar when the response omits its length.","locale":"en"},"4930":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-139","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`http.request` performs bounded UTF-8 HTTP through the host proxy. It accepts `url`, `method` (default `GET`; also `HEAD`, `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH`, `DELETE`, `OPTIONS`), `headers` as repeated `{name, value}` entries, optional text `body`, `maxBytes`, and `timeoutMs`. Bounds can only reduce the host defaults shown in the generated reference. The response is `{status, headers, body}`; even HTTP 404/500 returns a normal response. Invalid input is `INVALID_PARAMS`, denied hosts are `PERMISSION_DENIED`, and transport, size or UTF-8 failures are `REQUEST_FAILED`. Binary files belong in `download.fetch`. No host credentials are automatically added.","locale":"en"},"4931":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-140","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`python.ensure` installs a Python version the application supports where it is told; `git.clone` (whose destination must not exist) and `git.pull` (fast-forward only) use the application's Git, never prompt for credentials, and go through the application's proxy. All three answer a `taskId` at once (on a first run, while the application still prepares its Git, `git.*` first waits for it), are listed in the Task Center, end with `process.exit`, and stop with `process.cancel`.","locale":"en"},"4932":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-141","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`instances.scan` takes an absolute `path` and runs the application's instance discovery on that folder, as **Scan** does: it attaches the WebUI layouts it knows and every registered type's discovery signatures, and answers `attached` (the new instances' ids) and `warnings` (every result but a complete new instance, such as a folder that is already an instance, or a WebUI found without its Python, which is added but still needs installing; read them tolerantly). `instances.import` takes `type`, an absolute `path`, and optionally `name`, `componentPaths` (absolute or relative to `path`), and `values`, imports that folder as a ready instance of the type, and answers its `id`; the folder and every component folder must exist, and a folder an instance already uses is refused. Both answer when they finish and are not Task Center activities: a scan of a large folder can take a while, so answer the frame or panel call first, scan on a thread of your own, and report with `ui.progress`. Stopping the backend cancels a scan.","locale":"en"},"4933":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-142","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`ui.notify` (`info`, `warning`, `error`) and `ui.progress` (the same `id` updates the same entry) show as activities in the Task Center. Pass an optional `instanceId` of this extension's type to `ui.progress` to show the work in that instance’s management view. The id is local to that instance. Set `showOnHome: true` on progress reports to display them in the Home status dock while running.","locale":"en"},"4934":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-143","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`tools.list` lists the management tools with the `management` level each needs; `tools.call` takes a tool's `name` and `arguments` and answers the tool's own result (Interface Reference lists them all). A change asks the user in the application's window (up to a minute) naming the extension, and is recorded in the Task Center, so call it from a thread of your own, never inside a frame or panel call the application waits on. It cannot restart the extension's own row or one it depends on. Check each tool’s `builtInOnly` flag. Tools in the versions, extensions, models, and environment sections also work on extension types that adopt the relevant built-in management section. `install_extension` installs an add-on inside a WebUI instance. `list_host_extensions` lists desktop extensions installed in Hanafubuki.","locale":"en"},"4935":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-144","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"`events.subscribe` replaces the entire subscription. Send all desired topics together, or `{\"topics\":[]}` to unsubscribe. The host polls instance and server changes every 2 s. A successful write from Settings, a frame, or the backend sends the extension’s canonical settings as `event.params.data.values`. The `task` topic reports `{phase, activity}` for the Task Center activities started by this extension’s `tools.call` mutations (`start`, `progress`, `end`).","locale":"en"},"4936":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-145","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"To check whether this host implements an optional API, look for its name in `host.info`’s `capabilities.methods` or `capabilities.topics`. Check the extension’s grants separately in `initialize.permissions`.","locale":"en"},"4937":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-146","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#the-host-api","content":"**Notifications to the backend**: `event` (`topic` and `data`), `process.output` (`taskId`, `stream`, `data`), and `process.exit` (`taskId`, `status` of `exited`, `failed`, or `cancelled`, `exitCode`, `error`).","locale":"en"},"4938":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-147","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"Types that declare `provider = \"backend\"` use these callbacks for validation and planning. A declarative type with a backend can also supply instance-specific launch choices, declare an update check, or implement a custom management operation while keeping its static install and launch plans. Each callback has a 30 s timeout. See Instance types.","locale":"en"},"4939":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-148","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"Method","locale":"en"},"4940":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-149","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"Params","locale":"en"},"4941":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-150","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"Result","locale":"en"},"4942":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-151","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`provider.validateSpec`","locale":"en"},"4943":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-152","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`type`, `values` (the parameter values after the application's form checks), optional `instance`","locale":"en"},"4944":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-153","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`issues`: problems by parameter, `{}` when the values are good","locale":"en"},"4945":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-154","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`provider.installPlan`","locale":"en"},"4946":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-155","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`type`, `kind` (`install`: a first install or a retry; `reinstall`: repeat installation; `update`: a ready instance's update), `recreateComponents` (selected component names), `values`, `instance`","locale":"en"},"4947":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-156","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`steps`: steps written as a descriptor's `install.steps` are","locale":"en"},"4948":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-157","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`provider.launchOptions`","locale":"en"},"4949":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-158","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`type`, `instance`","locale":"en"},"4950":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-159","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`options` maps each launch field’s `optionsSource` to an array of string choices.","locale":"en"},"4951":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-160","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`provider.launchPlan`","locale":"en"},"4952":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-161","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`type`, `role`, `port`, `values`, `launchValues`, `instance`","locale":"en"},"4953":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-162","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`argv`, `env`; placeholders and `{port}` allowed","locale":"en"},"4954":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-163","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`provider.checkUpdate`","locale":"en"},"4955":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-164","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`type`, `values` (the instance's parameter values), `instance`","locale":"en"},"4956":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-165","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`hasUpdate`: whether an applicable update exists (`false` for an instance its values pin to a version); optional `current`, `latest` (versions as the user reads them) and `notes`. Optional `components` lists `{name, current?, latest?, hasUpdate}` for each component.","locale":"en"},"4957":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-166","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`provider.manage.<operation>`","locale":"en"},"4958":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-167","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`type`, `operation`, `instance`, `values` (validated form values, including defaults)","locale":"en"},"4959":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-168","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"Optional `notice` and `steps` in install-plan format","locale":"en"},"4960":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-169","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"A provider callback’s `instance` contains `id` and a `components` map. For example, `instance.components.core.path` is the current native path for `core`, and `external: true` marks a user-owned directory. Read that path to inspect a component and use `{component.core}` in a returned plan. `provider.validateSpec` receives this object when an existing instance is reinstalled with new values. Creation has no instance yet.","locale":"en"},"4961":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-170","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"`provider.checkUpdate` is asked only for a type that declares `update-check`, by the card's \"check for updates\" and by the daily update check, which asks once per instance, one after another. It is read-only: the instance may be running, and the check must change nothing. Answer fast: cache or share what you fetch (a release list) between instances, since a call past 30 s fails and three in a row end the channel. An error you answer is shown on that instance as its check's error.","locale":"en"},"4962":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-171","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"An operation of its own runs while the instance is stopped, under exclusive maintenance of the instance; it is refused while the instance runs or while another maintenance holds it. Besides the instance's management page, MCP clients and the built-in assistant can run it through the management MCP server's `run_instance_operation` tool, which external clients can run without confirmation when granted full access (Management MCP server).","locale":"en"},"4963":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-172","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"Call `instances.configuration` with `{ id }` to read one instance of your own types. Its `values` and `launchValues` maps include declared, non-secret install and launch fields with their defaults. Credentials are redacted. The `valuesTruncated` and `launchValuesTruncated` flags report whether the maps reached the host's limits.","locale":"en"},"4964":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-173","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#provider-callbacks","content":"Panel and frame calls (`ui.panel.query`, `ui.panel.action`, `ui.frame.call`), the UI state's `ui.state` and `ui.call`, and the `ui.frame.emit` and `ui.emit` notifications are on Interface. Those two are notifications only: sent as a request, each is answered `METHOD_NOT_FOUND`.","locale":"en"},"4965":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-174","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#publishing-tools-on-the-management-mcp-endpoint","content":"An extension with a backend declares its tools and their arguments in the manifest. For example, `com.example.greeter` can declare:","locale":"en"},"4966":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-175","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#publishing-tools-on-the-management-mcp-endpoint","content":"While the extension is enabled, the assistant and external clients can discover `ext_com_example_greeter__greet` on the `hanafubuki` server. Handle `service.greet` in the backend, or override `Backend.service` in Python. The management endpoint sets `caller` to `mcp:hanafubuki` and passes the client's argument object as `params`. Validate the arguments and return a JSON result. Disabling the extension withdraws its tools.","locale":"en"},"4967":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-176","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#publishing-tools-on-the-management-mcp-endpoint","content":"Publishing an MCP tool only needs the declaration and its backend handler. The `management` permission applies when the backend calls the host’s management catalog. Add the method to `backend.exports` if other extensions should also be able to call it through `services.call`.","locale":"en"},"4968":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-177","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#services-between-extensions","content":"An extension may list in `[backend] exports` the methods other extensions may call. An extension that depends on it calls:","locale":"en"},"4969":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-178","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#services-between-extensions","content":"The application forwards it to the target's backend as a `service.models.list` call with `{ \"caller\": \"<caller id>\", \"params\": … }`, and answers the caller with what the target answered.","locale":"en"},"4970":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-179","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#services-between-extensions","content":"A caller whose manifest does not depend on the target, or a method the target does not export, gets `PERMISSION_DENIED`.","locale":"en"},"4971":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-180","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#services-between-extensions","content":"A target whose backend does not run answers `UNAVAILABLE`.","locale":"en"},"4972":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-181","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#services-between-extensions","content":"A target that fails or ends mid-call fails that call alone; the target's fault is its own, never the caller's.","locale":"en"},"4973":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-182","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#read-only-host-preferences","content":"A backend with management read access can call `get_app_runtime_status` through `tools.call` with `{\"runtime\":\"mirrors\"}` to read the model download source (`modelSource`) and the Micromamba mirror preference (`useMicromamba`).","locale":"en"},"4974":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-183","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#read-only-host-preferences","content":"`host.preferences {}` returns `{locale, instancesDir, mirrors, network}` without extra permission. Locale, the native instance directory, and mirrors have the same meanings and null defaults as `host.info`; `network` contains only `proxyMode` (`system`, `manual`, `disabled`) and `proxyConfigured`. Configured does not mean reachable or active. The snapshot contains no proxy URL or credentials and cannot change application settings.","locale":"en"},"4975":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-184","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#read-only-host-preferences","content":"Subscribe to the `preferences` topic with `events.subscribe`, then read the current snapshot with `host.preferences`. Each matching `event` carries the complete same typed snapshot in `params.data`. A successful related host settings change publishes it; a proxy address change also publishes when the public fields are unchanged. An unrelated or failed settings save does not. Restarted backends subscribe and read again; there is no replay. `settings` continues to refer only to the extension's own settings.","locale":"en"},"4976":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-185","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#read-only-host-preferences","content":"The generated topic reference lists actual payload fields and permissions directly from the protocol registry.","locale":"en"},"4977":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-186","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#runtime-tools-and-diagnostic-documents","content":"`runtime.status {tool?}` reads the existing readiness snapshot without starting preparation. Omitting `tool` lists `python`, `git` and `micromamba`. Each item has `tool`, `status` (`notReady`, `preparing`, `ready`, `failed`, `unavailable`) and optional native `path`, verified `version`/command output and `error`. Reading needs no extra permission. A version is not guaranteed to be a semantic-version string.","locale":"en"},"4978":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-187","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#runtime-tools-and-diagnostic-documents","content":"`runtime.resolve {tool}` requires `processes = true`. It waits for the host's existing preparation and returns `{tool, path, version}`; an unavailable module or failed preparation returns the real reason. Backend stop cancels the wait. SDK calls have no short client timer that truncates preparation. Use the returned native executable path with `process.run`; no tool deletion or runtime configuration API is exposed.","locale":"en"},"4979":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-188","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#runtime-tools-and-diagnostic-documents","content":"For documents about one instance, announce `diagnostics.collectInstance`. The host sends `{id}` for an instance of your extension’s type and writes the returned documents beneath that instance’s `extensions/<id>/` directory. Rust implements `InstanceDiagnostics` and returns it from `Handler::instance_diagnostics`. Python overrides `diagnostics_collect_instance(host, instance_id)`.","locale":"en"},"4980":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-189","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#runtime-tools-and-diagnostic-documents","content":"A backend announcing `diagnostics` in `initialize.hooks` answers `diagnostics.collect {}` with `{documents: [{name, text}]}`. Names are single filenames, not paths; text may be plain text or serialized JSON. The host writes under `app/extensions/<id>/` in its diagnostic workspace. Do not include credentials. Disabling or uninstalling the extension removes its contribution. Collection failures and timeouts become bundle issues while other contributors continue. The generated method reference shows the actual call timeout, maximum document count and total byte limit.","locale":"en"},"4981":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-190","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#runtime-tools-and-diagnostic-documents","content":"The Python manifest above is copied from the complete `configuration-python` package. Its real `backend.py` uses the SDK's initialize/ping/shutdown loop. Package validation checks its files but does not prepare managed Python or start the backend; a separate local protocol smoke is not desktop acceptance. The native command-map fragment describes a choice of executables, not a complete installed package: build and package the greeter example for that path.","locale":"en"},"4982":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends-191","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-backends#ask-a-question-from-a-background-operation","content":"Call `ui.prompt` with a fresh local ID. For example, a Python backend can call\n`host.ui_prompt({\"id\": \"model-name\", \"kind\": \"text\", \"message\": \"Model name?\"})`.\nThe call returns on acceptance. The main window identifies the extension and\nshows the question. Handle the later `ui.prompt.result` notification by its\n`id`. An accepted text answer contains the entered string, including an empty\nstring. A dismissal has `accepted: false`. The `text` field is null for a\ndismissal or a `confirm` question. A second outstanding question returns\n`BUSY`. Stopping or restarting the backend withdraws its pending question.\nManagement mutations continue to ask for the host's own confirmation.","locale":"en"},"4983":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","type":"page","content":"Instance Types","breadcrumbs":["Hanafubuki Documentation","Extension Development"],"tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","locale":"en"},"4984":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-0","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","content":"Declare instance types in descriptor files - components, runtime, install parameters, install and update steps, placeholders, server roles, management operations, discovery, and types a backend provides.","locale":"en"},"4985":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-1","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#a-complete-example","content":"A complete example","locale":"en"},"4986":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-2","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#top-level-keys","content":"Top-level keys","locale":"en"},"4987":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-3","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#components","content":"Components","locale":"en"},"4988":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-4","page_id":"/en/d