es-58","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#top-level-keys","content":"No","locale":"en"},"5043":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-59","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#top-level-keys","content":"`steps`: a ready instance's update steps","locale":"en"},"5044":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-60","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#top-level-keys","content":"`servers`","locale":"en"},"5045":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-61","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#top-level-keys","content":"No","locale":"en"},"5046":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-62","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#top-level-keys","content":"The server roles","locale":"en"},"5047":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-63","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#top-level-keys","content":"`management`","locale":"en"},"5048":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-64","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#top-level-keys","content":"No","locale":"en"},"5049":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-65","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#top-level-keys","content":"`operations`: the management operations its instances offer; `adopts`: the built-in type whose management they take (below)","locale":"en"},"5050":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-66","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#top-level-keys","content":"`discovery`","locale":"en"},"5051":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-67","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#top-level-keys","content":"No","locale":"en"},"5052":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-68","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#top-level-keys","content":"Signatures that recognize existing installations; see Discovery","locale":"en"},"5053":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-69","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#components","content":"A component may set `defaultPath = \"assets/weights\"` to place it below the install root. Explicit component locations take precedence. Set `[terminal].cwd` to a component path such as `\"{component.core}/tools\"` for an instance terminal that opens in the CLI directory. The default is the core directory.","locale":"en"},"5054":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-70","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#components","content":"Components are the instance's directories. By default each one is a subdirectory of the install directory with its own name, `<install dir>/<name>`; users may place a component elsewhere when they create the instance. Steps and commands refer to a component with `{component.<name>}` and never write a path. `core` always exists, and server processes run in it.","locale":"en"},"5055":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-71","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#components","content":"To let users reuse an existing installation, declare which component can point to it. For example, this type can use an existing Node.js directory:","locale":"en"},"5056":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-72","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#components","content":"A newly created instance owns its components. When the user selects an external directory in **Paths**, the host checks that the directory and the optional platform-specific executable exist. Discovery can also mark components external during import. The managed `python` component always belongs to the instance.","locale":"en"},"5057":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-73","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#components","content":"Deleting or reinstalling the instance preserves external directories. The host refuses `git-clone`, `unpack` and `download` with `unpackTo` anywhere inside one. File downloads, `write-file` and `git-pull` can still modify its contents. A backend planning a `run` step should check the component's `external` flag before asking its own program to change files.","locale":"en"},"5058":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-74","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#components","content":"A component can declare `rebuildable`. This example offers a Node.js rebuild switch and hides the Core switch. By default, only `core` and `python` offer switches. Each switch starts off and applies to one install. External directories cannot be rebuilt.","locale":"en"},"5059":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-75","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#runtime","content":"Form","locale":"en"},"5060":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-76","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#runtime","content":"Description","locale":"en"},"5061":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-77","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#runtime","content":"`{ kind = \"none\" }`","locale":"en"},"5062":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-78","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#runtime","content":"No runtime (the default)","locale":"en"},"5063":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-79","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#runtime","content":"`{ kind = \"python\", version = \"3.11\" }`","locale":"en"},"5064":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-80","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#runtime","content":"The application's managed Python; the version must be one the application installs (`3.10` to `3.15`), and `python` must be a declared component","locale":"en"},"5065":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-81","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#runtime","content":"`{ kind = \"system-binary\", name = \"node\" }`","locale":"en"},"5066":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-82","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#runtime","content":"A program on the system; `name` is a program name without a path separator","locale":"en"},"5067":{"id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types-83","page_id":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/en/docs/extension-dev-instance-types#text","content":"`displayName`, `description`, a component's `label`, and a parameter's 